Log which ground-lock gate rejects a landing surface.            (SIM-27 step 1)

MEASUREMENT ONLY, AND TEMPORARY. This patch adds no behaviour: it prints what
FastPhysicsEngine already decided, then leaves every decision exactly as it was. It exists
because SIM-27's central claim is READ OUT OF THIS FILE and has never been observed running.

The claim: only one branch stops a descent -- the ground lock, which zeroes velocity, snaps
the body to the contact point and calls body.setGrounded(true). Reaching it needs all of

  A  the contact must not be treated as separating   (normal . next_velocity < 0)
  B  the contact must look like ground               (|normal_body.z()| ~= 1, kAxisTolerance 0.25)
     ... and like a landing                          (z_vel dominates x and y)
  C  ground lock must be enabled                     (EnableGroundLock, default true)

So "is this the ground?" is answered ENTIRELY from the collision normal. On CitySample the
vehicle rests on FastGeoSurrogateActor_0, a coarse far-field proxy, and the leading hypothesis
is that its normal is off-axis enough to fail B -- which would leave the actor jammed on
geometry while the integrator keeps integrating downward, the 107.9 m split SIM-27 records.
Blocks has a flat plane and cannot express the fault at all.

WHICH GATE ACTUALLY FAILS IS UNMEASURED, and A and B imply different fixes. This prints one
throttled line per gate so a single seeded flight answers it.

Read the result with:  docker logs sim-unreal 2>&1 | grep SIM27

The logger is static because getNextKinematicsOnCollision is static and cannot reach the
existing throttledLogOutput (that needs clock() and last_message_time, both instance state).
It throttles on wall time, not a call count: a 1-in-N counter can skip the transition, which
is the only moment that matters.

EACH GATE THROTTLES SEPARATELY, and the first flight (2026-08-29) is why. With one shared
timer the noisy gate starves the rare one: gates B and C are logged microseconds apart in the
SAME call, so B always reset the timer and C was ALWAYS suppressed -- 386 lines, not one of
them gate=C, from a landing that demonstrably ground-locked. Counts across gates are still
only indicative, never a frequency: the throttle samples the first call in each window.

NOT APPLIED BY DEFAULT -- it lives under experimental/, outside the *.patch glob that
build_blocks.sh and convert_world.sh apply. Apply it deliberately, measure, then remove it.

